Fillets: a stripe is under another one's ball (patch 0700) only where its
edges lie on the ball's side of that blend's other face, not past it
(mitcad#840).

The case: a wall with a slot whose floor is flush with the top of a ledge
in front of the wall; at each corner where the slot's side wall meets the
wall's front face, the slot floor's edge along the side wall and the
ledge's edge along the wall's front (2.25 long) are rounded with 1, the
convex edge between the side wall and the front left sharp. The bodies
stored in the file have both roundings running on past the corner by the
radius, the two quarter cylinders cut by each other.

0700's JoinOverNarrowFaces drops a stripe whose edges all lie under
another stripe's ball (edgesUnderBlend): each point of its edges within 4
radii of that blend's contact on the common face, inside one of its pieces,
on the side of the contact toward the blend's other contact. The slot
floor's rounding runs on over the ledge's top for its corner (its analytic
piece's contact up to the end of the face), and the ledge's edge lies 1 to
3.25 from that contact, on its side toward the side wall, but past the side
wall's plane: no point of the region the blend fills. Its stripe was
dropped, its rounding missing, and the slot's rounding ended at the wall's
front face by its one-stripe corner (1.349 mm3 of material short at the
design's four such corners).

- edgesUnderBlend: a point counts only where it lies on the ball's side of
  the blend's other face's tangent plane at the other contact, within a
  hundredth of the radius (the ball of the section through the point's
  projection on the contact, ballAt). The region a blend fills lies between
  its contacts and the corner of its two faces; the edges of a narrow face
  under a ball rolling over it (0700's case) lie there.

Upstream: new (fixes Mitcad's patch 0700).
